{"region":"Texas and South Central","version":1,"model":"model.onnx","inputSize":256,"resizeRatio":1.14,"mean":[0.485,0.456,0.406],"std":[0.229,0.224,0.225],"priorWeight":0.5,"species":[{"scientific":"Carya illinoinensis","common":"Pecan","family":"Walnut family (Juglandaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"pinnate","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no","nut":"splits"},"features":["Compound leaves with 9 to 17 curved, sickle-shaped leaflets","Nuts in a thin husk that splits into 4 sections when ripe","Texas state tree"],"lookalikes":[["Juglans nigra","Black walnut husks are round green balls that don't split open, and its leaflets are straighter and more numerous (15 to 23)."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Celtis laevigata","common":"Sugarberry","family":"Hemp family (Cannabaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Smooth gray bark covered in corky warts","Lance-shaped leaves with a long tapered tip and uneven base","Small orange to purple berries eaten by birds"],"lookalikes":[["Ulmus americana","Elm bark is furrowed without corky warts, and elm leaves have double-toothed edges."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Cercis canadensis","common":"Eastern redbud","family":"Pea family (Fabaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Heart-shaped leaves with smooth edges","Pink-purple flowers along bare branches in early spring","Flat brown pea-like pods"],"lookalikes":[["Triadica sebifera","Chinese tallow leaves are diamond-shaped with a pointed tip, not heart-shaped."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Dermatophyllum secundiflorum","common":"Texas mountain laurel","family":"Pea family (Fabaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"pinnate","lobed":"no","evergreen":"yes","thorns":"no"},"features":["Glossy evergreen compound leaves with 5 to 11 rounded leaflets","Purple flower clusters in early spring that smell like grape soda","Woody pods with bright red seeds"],"lookalikes":[["Ilex vomitoria","Yaupon leaves are simple (one blade per stem), not compound."]],"notes":["Seeds are highly poisonous if chewed. Keep them away from children and pets."]},{"scientific":"Diospyros texana","common":"Texas persimmon","family":"Ebony family (Ebenaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no","bark":"smooth"},"features":["Smooth, pale gray bark that peels to show white and pink patches, like crape myrtle","Small, leathery leaves with rolled-under edges","Small black fruit in late summer; usually a shrub or small multi-trunk tree"],"lookalikes":[["Lagerstroemia indica","Crape myrtle leaves are opposite or nearly so, and it has showy pink, red or white flower clusters."],["Diospyros virginiana","Common persimmon has dark, blocky bark and large orange fruit."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Diospyros virginiana","common":"Common persimmon","family":"Ebony family (Ebenaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no","bark":"blocky"},"features":["Dark bark broken into small square blocks, like alligator skin","Oval leaves 3 to 6 inches with smooth edges","Orange fruit about 1 to 2 inches, sweet only when fully ripe and soft"],"lookalikes":[["Diospyros texana","Texas persimmon has smooth peeling gray bark and small black fruit."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Fraxinus pennsylvanica","common":"Green ash","family":"Olive family (Oleaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"opposite","leaf":"pinnate","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Compound leaves with 5 to 9 leaflets, opposite each other on the twig","Bark with tight diamond-shaped ridges","Narrow, paddle-shaped winged seeds in clusters"],"lookalikes":[["Juglans nigra","Black walnut leaves and twigs alternate along the branch and have many more leaflets."],["Carya illinoinensis","Pecan leaves alternate along the branch."]],"notes":["Threatened by the emerald ash borer, which has been found in North and East Texas. Report D-shaped exit holes in the bark to the Texas A&M Forest Service."]},{"scientific":"Ilex vomitoria","common":"Yaupon holly","family":"Holly family (Aquifoliaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"yes","thorns":"no"},"features":["Small, oval evergreen leaves with rounded teeth","Bright red berries on female plants in fall and winter","Smooth, light gray bark; usually a shrub or small tree"],"lookalikes":[["Quercus virginiana","Live oak leaves are larger with smooth edges, and it grows into a large tree."]],"notes":["Berries can cause vomiting if eaten; keep them away from children and pets."]},{"scientific":"Juglans nigra","common":"Black walnut","family":"Walnut family (Juglandaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"pinnate","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no","nut":"round"},"features":["Compound leaves with 15 to 23 leaflets, the end leaflet often missing","Round green fruit that doesn't split open and stains hands brown","Dark, deeply furrowed bark in a diamond pattern"],"lookalikes":[["Carya illinoinensis","Pecan husks split into 4 sections and its leaflets are fewer and curved."],["Fraxinus pennsylvanica","Green ash leaves and twigs are opposite each other, not alternate."]],"notes":["Roots and leaves release juglone, which harms many garden plants nearby."]},{"scientific":"Juniperus ashei","common":"Ashe juniper","family":"Cypress family (Cupressaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"scale","leaf":"scale","lobed":"no","evergreen":"yes","thorns":"no","trunks":"many"},"features":["Tiny scale-like leaves on cord-like twigs","Often several trunks from the base, forming a rounded shrubby tree","Grows on limestone hills of the Edwards Plateau; often called \"mountain cedar\"","Blue berry-like cones, slightly larger than eastern red cedar's"],"lookalikes":[["Juniperus virginiana","Eastern red cedar usually has one straight trunk and a narrow cone shape, and is more common east of the Hill Country."]],"notes":["A major source of winter \"cedar fever\" pollen in December and January."]},{"scientific":"Juniperus virginiana","common":"Eastern red cedar","family":"Cypress family (Cupressaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"scale","leaf":"scale","lobed":"no","evergreen":"yes","thorns":"no","trunks":"one"},"features":["Tiny scale-like leaves; young shoots may have short prickly needles","Usually one straight trunk with a narrow pyramid or column shape","Reddish, thin, shreddy bark"],"lookalikes":[["Juniperus ashei","Ashe juniper usually has several trunks from the base and grows mostly on Hill Country limestone."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Lagerstroemia indica","common":"Crape myrtle","family":"Loosestrife family (Lythraceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"opposite","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no","bark":"smooth"},"features":["Smooth, peeling bark in tan, gray and cinnamon patches","Large clusters of crinkled pink, red, purple or white flowers in summer","Planted ornamental, often with multiple trunks"],"lookalikes":[["Diospyros texana","Texas persimmon leaves alternate along the twig and it has small black fruit instead of showy flowers."]],"notes":["Not native; widely planted in yards and along streets."]},{"scientific":"Liquidambar styraciflua","common":"Sweetgum","family":"Sweetgum family (Altingiaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"yes","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Star-shaped leaves with 5 to 7 pointed lobes","Spiky round seed balls (\"gumballs\") that hang on through winter","Twigs often have corky wings; brilliant fall color"],"lookalikes":[["Platanus occidentalis","Sycamore leaves are larger with shallower lobes, and its bark peels in white and tan patches."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Maclura pomifera","common":"Osage orange (bois d'arc)","family":"Mulberry family (Moraceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"yes"},"features":["Large, bumpy, green softball-sized fruit (\"horse apples\")","Sharp thorns on the twigs","Glossy, oval leaves with a long tip; orange inner bark and milky sap"],"lookalikes":[["Celtis laevigata","Sugarberry has no thorns, warty bark and small berries."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Magnolia grandiflora","common":"Southern magnolia","family":"Magnolia family (Magnoliaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"yes","thorns":"no"},"features":["Large, thick, glossy leaves, often rusty-brown and fuzzy underneath","Huge white fragrant flowers in late spring and summer","Cone-like fruit with bright red seeds"],"lookalikes":[["Quercus virginiana","Live oak leaves are much smaller (1 to 4 inches) and not glossy."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Melia azedarach","common":"Chinaberry","family":"Mahogany family (Meliaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"bipinnate","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Large, twice-compound leaves with toothed leaflets","Fragrant lilac flowers in spring","Round yellow berries that hang on through winter"],"lookalikes":[["Prosopis glandulosa","Honey mesquite has thorns and much smaller leaflets."]],"notes":["Invasive in Texas.","Berries are poisonous to people and pets if eaten."]},{"scientific":"Pinus taeda","common":"Loblolly pine","family":"Pine family (Pinaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"needles","leaf":"needle","lobed":"no","evergreen":"yes","thorns":"no"},"features":["Needles 6 to 9 inches long, usually in bundles of 3","Cones 3 to 6 inches with sharp prickles on the scales","Tall, straight trunk with scaly, reddish-brown plates"],"lookalikes":[["Taxodium distichum","Bald cypress needles are short, flat and soft, and it drops them in fall."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Platanus occidentalis","common":"American sycamore","family":"Plane-tree family (Platanaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"yes","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Bark peels in patches, leaving white, tan and green camouflage colors","Large leaves with 3 to 5 shallow lobes","Single round seed balls hanging on long stems"],"lookalikes":[["Liquidambar styraciflua","Sweetgum leaves are star-shaped with deeper lobes and its seed balls are spiky."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Populus deltoides","common":"Eastern cottonwood","family":"Willow family (Salicaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Triangular leaves with coarse teeth and flattened stems that make them flutter","Cottony seeds released in late spring","Very large, fast-growing tree near water"],"lookalikes":[["Salix nigra","Black willow leaves are long and narrow."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Prosopis glandulosa","common":"Honey mesquite","family":"Pea family (Fabaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"bipinnate","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"yes"},"features":["Feathery leaves split into two (sometimes more) branches of small leaflets","Paired straight thorns on the twigs","Long, flat, yellowish bean pods in summer"],"lookalikes":[["Melia azedarach","Chinaberry leaves are much larger with toothed leaflets, and it has no thorns."]],"notes":["Now classified as Neltuma glandulosa by some sources."]},{"scientific":"Quercus macrocarpa","common":"Bur oak","family":"Beech family (Fagaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"yes","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Large leaves with a deep cut near the middle, giving a narrow waist","Very large acorns with a deep, fringed (mossy) cap","Thick, deeply furrowed bark; corky ridges on young twigs"],"lookalikes":[["Quercus stellata","Post oak acorns are small with a plain scaly cap, and its leaves are cross-shaped."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Quercus nigra","common":"Water oak","family":"Beech family (Fagaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Spoon-shaped leaves, wide at the tip and narrow at the base, sometimes with 3 shallow lobes","Holds some leaves into winter, then drops them","Common in wet bottomlands and yards in East Texas"],"lookalikes":[["Quercus virginiana","Live oak keeps all its leaves through winter and the leaves are oval, not spoon-shaped."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Quercus shumardii","common":"Shumard oak","family":"Beech family (Fagaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"yes","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Deeply cut lobes that end in sharp bristle tips","Glossy, smooth leaves with deep, rounded gaps between the lobes","Red to orange fall color"],"lookalikes":[["Quercus stellata","Post oak lobes are rounded with no bristle tips."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Quercus stellata","common":"Post oak","family":"Beech family (Fagaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"yes","evergreen":"no","thorns":"no"},"features":["Leaves with two large middle lobes that make a cross or Maltese-cross shape","Thick, rough, star-shaped hairs underneath the leaf","Small acorns with a bowl-shaped, scaly cap covering about a third of the nut"],"lookalikes":[["Quercus macrocarpa","Bur oak acorns are large with a deep, fringed cap, and its leaves have a narrow waist near the middle."]],"notes":[]},{"scientific":"Quercus virginiana","common":"Live oak","family":"Beech family (Fagaceae)","traits":{"arrangement":"alternate","leaf":"simple","lobed":"no","evergreen":"yes","thorns":"no"},"features":["Leathery, oval leaves with smooth edges, dark green on top and pale underneath","Keeps its leaves through winter","Wide, spreading crown, often wider than tall","Small, dark, long acorns, often in clusters"],"lookalikes":[["Quercus nigra","Water oak leaves are wider at the tip (spoon-shaped) and the tree drops most leaves by late winter."]